meta {
  name: 02 Create payment
  type: http
  seq: 2
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/api/payments
  body: json
  auth: none
}

headers {
  Authorization: Bearer {{accessToken}}
  Idempotency-Key: {{idempotencyKey}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "start": 5,
    "pro": 5
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setEnvVar("paymentId", res.getBody().id);
  }
}

assert {
  res.status: eq 201
  res.body.status: eq PENDING
  res.body.totalSeats: eq 10
  res.body.amountXaf: eq 72500
  res.body.lines[0].tier: eq START
  res.body.lines[1].tier: eq PRO
}

docs {
  Records an intent to pay for a mix of seats. NO MONEY MOVES, and nothing is
  granted.

  Check `Center Subscription / 01 Get subscription` before and after:
  `paidUntil` is unchanged and `seatsHeld` is unchanged. Check `center_seats`
  too — the row count and every `unit_price_xaf` must be untouched. Only a
  verified Notch Pay event may move those, and that arrives in Phase 7.
  `status` stays PENDING because nothing in this phase can advance it.

  ## The breakdown is the invoice

  The response carries a line per tier, each with the price that applied at
  the time. That is what keeps an old invoice truthful after the list price
  moves: 5 Start at 4,500 plus 5 Pro at 10,000 is 72,500, and it still reads
  that way next year. `amountXaf` always equals the sum of the lines, and a
  database CHECK enforces that each line's amount follows from its own two
  factors.

  ## Set the key first

  Put any unique value in `{{idempotencyKey}}` in the environment — a uuid is
  the intended shape. Change it whenever you want a genuinely NEW payment.

  ## The four things to try, in this order

  1. SEND IT TWICE WITHOUT CHANGING ANYTHING.
     Both return 201, and `id` is the SAME both times. A double-clicked button
     is one intent, not two payments. Confirm with `03 Payment history`: one
     row, not two.

  2. SAME KEY, DIFFERENT MIX. Resend with `{"pro": 10}` and the same key ->
     409 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REUSED.
     Handing back the original would tell you that you had bought Pro seats
     while the record says Start, and that disagreement would only surface
     when reconciling against the provider.

  3. SAME KEY, SAME TOTAL, DIFFERENT MIX. Resend `{"start": 10}` with the same
     key -> 409, even though both mixes are ten seats.
     This is the collision worth checking by hand: the fingerprint covers
     every tier, not the total. Hashing the total alone would let ten Start
     pass as five Start plus five Pro at a different price.

  4. NEW KEY, SAME MIX -> a genuinely new payment with a new id. The key is
     what identifies the attempt, not the body.

  ### And the retry that must NOT be refused

  Create a payment, then change that center's stamped Start price by hand
  (`UPDATE center_seats SET unit_price_xaf = 4000 WHERE ...`), then resend the
  ORIGINAL body with the ORIGINAL key. It must return the original payment at
  the original price — not a 409.

  The fingerprint covers what the caller asked for, never the price, because
  the price is the server's answer to the question rather than part of it. If
  this ever 409s, a client following the documented contract would answer it
  with a fresh key and create a SECOND payment for one intent, which is the
  exact failure the unique index exists to prevent.

  ## Without a key

  Remove the `Idempotency-Key` header -> 400 IDEMPOTENCY_KEY_REQUIRED.

  It is required rather than generated server-side on purpose: a key the
  server invents makes every retry a fresh payment. Empty and whitespace-only
  keys are refused for the same reason.

  ## The price is not an input, here either

  All of these -> 400:

  - `{"start": 10, "unitPriceXaf": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "amountXaf": 1}`
  - `{"start": 10, "status": "SUCCEEDED"}`
  - `{"start": 10, "lines": []}`
  - `{"seats": 10}` — the pre-tier shape.

  That `status` one matters most. Without it a client could ask for a payment
  that claims to have already succeeded.

  ## An empty body costs nothing

  `{}` -> 400 SEAT_MIX_EMPTY, and it does NOT spend a slot of the 20-per-hour
  rate limit. Send it twenty times, then send a real mix: it must still go
  through. Every tier field is optional, so an empty body clears validation
  and reaches the handler — charging for it would let twenty empty submits
  lock a center out of the one route it has to be able to reach.

  ## THE PROFILE GATE

  A center must have finished its profile before it can be charged. Clear its
  country, city and manager phone:

      UPDATE centers SET country = NULL, city = NULL WHERE id = '...';
      UPDATE center_users SET phone = NULL WHERE center_id = '...';

  Then resend -> 403 CENTER_PROFILE_INCOMPLETE with
  `missing: ["country", "city", "phone"]`.

  Fill them back in via `Center Profile / 02 Patch me` and it goes through.
  Clear only the phone and the refusal names only `["phone"]` — the list is
  what a dashboard renders its remaining checklist from, so it must be exact.

  `01 Quote seats` stays open throughout. Looking at a price is not a
  commitment, and the quote is the page that explains why finishing the
  profile is worth it.

  ## While BLOCKED

  This works. It is the point of the whole road-out design: a lapsed center
  must be able to pay. If this ever returns 403 SUBSCRIPTION_INACTIVE, someone
  has added CenterSubscriptionGuard to the payments controller —
  `test/center-blocked-surface.spec.ts` should have failed first.
}
